WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:06.560
I bet you've seen this drop stitch pattern
in ready to wear sweaters.

2
00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.760
If you're like me,
you've wondered how it's done.

3
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.320
The thought of counting and transferring

4
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.080
stitches and reforming stitches,
it's really overwhelming.

5
00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.760
But put your ribber to work and make
yourself a cheat sheet

6
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.400
and you'll be amazed at how much fun this
is to knit on any machine with a ribber.

7
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.360
I'm thinking of using it as a substitute

8
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.280
for the front edges of our
Elongated Stitches Cardigan.

9
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.680
It would also be beautiful
for an all all-overover sweater design.

10
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.840
Let's get started.

11
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.720
Use a fine yarn for this pattern.

12
00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:49.600
I found that I really
struggled with thicker yarn.

13
00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:52.560
Plan for a multiple of 4 plus 3

14
00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.400
stitches.
This will give you columns of three purl

15
00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:59.520
stitches on each side
of your knitted piece.

16
00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.000
We don't want to be working the drop
stitches right along the edges.

17
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.080
Because this is a double bed stitch

18
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.280
pattern, we'll be using both beds
and we'll need to hang the Ribber comb

19
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.880
at some point.
Here is the easiest way I found.

20
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:24.240
Raise your Ribber, pitch H and put
the needles from both beds into work.

21
00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.280
And knit one row with ravle cord across all

22
00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.960
of the needles. So you get your zigzag row in there.

23
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.400
Insert the teeth of the comb
in the ribber loops.

24
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:42.040
Uncouple the ribber and pass it across,
so the ribber stitchers drop off.

25
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.360
If you've inserted the comb correctly,

26
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.760
the comb will hang in those ribber stitches and
provide nice even weight on your work.

27
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:57.760
With the cast on comb in place,
use any cast on that you like to start

28
00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.520
your knitting. The cast on must be
stretchy for this stitch pattern.

29
00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.840
I tried to loose crochet cast on over
the needles on the main bed,

30
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.600
but honestly, I found this to be
a bit tight in my finished piece.

31
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:14.960
In the future,

32
00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:21.000
I think I'll cast on with waste yarn and ravel
cord and then finish the edge by hand.

33
00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:25.240
Did I mention that the cast
on needs to be really stretchy?

34
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.880
OK, one way or another,
we've got some stitches on the needles

35
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:34.640
with the main yarn on the main bed,
we've knit one row.

36
00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.320
Next we'll set up the Ribber
needle arrangement.

37
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.440
No, don't go all cross-eyed on me.

38
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:43.040
The goal here is to get our beds set up

39
00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:48.080
in this needle arrangement.
The main bed with 3 needles and work.

40
00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:50.400
1 needle out of work all the way across

41
00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:56.160
the bed. And on the ribber will have
the two needles on either side of these

42
00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:02.800
and B needles in working position
and two needles out of work between them.

43
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.640
Accomplish this however you like,
but here's a suggestion.

44
00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.840
Raise your ribber and swing to P.

45
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.800
This will ailign the needles
directly across from each other.

46
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.200
Transfer every 4th stitch
down to the ribber.

47
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.760
Now, having the ribber set in this

48
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:26.200
position makes this transfer
easier, goes directly down.

49
00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:34.000
Next, we want 2stitches stiches on the ribber,

50
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.440
so we need to create a new stitch
by picking up the butt of one

51
00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.000
of the stitches on the main bed
and transferring it down to the ribber.

52
00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.880
Swing your ribber to H
to make things easier.

53
00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:04.560
At this point, your needle
bed should look like this.

54
00:04:05.800 --> 00:04:10.560
Keep the Ribber set to H
for the whole pattern.

55
00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:17.360
Identify your A and B needles and mark
your needle bed or create a cheat sheet.

56
00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:22.680
Keep in mind that we're going to be
dropping stitches, alternately in sets.

57
00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.720
Can we finally start knitting?

58
00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:30.920
The first set is the A set.
Put the empty out of work needles

59
00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:37.240
identified as A into working position.
Knit 10 rows.

60
00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:51.120
And then drop those A stitches and put
the A needles into out of work position.

61
00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.640
The second set or the B set,
you can put the emptied out of work

62
00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:06.120
needles identified as B in working
position knit 10 rows.

63
00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:21.680
Drop the B stitches and put the B
needles in out of work position.

64
00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:28.960
Will be repeating these two steps, these A
and B steps for the length of the piece.

65
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.560
When you're done knitting the length

66
00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:45.120
of your piece, transfer all the ribber
stitches up to the main bed.

67
00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:47.000
Now both of those Ribber stitches are

68
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.200
going to double up and fill
the emptied A and B needles.

69
00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:58.520
Knit one row with the very loosest
tension and blooselyindoff loosley.

70
00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:03.760
Did I mention that the cast
on in the Bindoff must be loose?

71
00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:05.760
Now for the fun part.

72
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:09.360
Drop all those stitches and form
the pattern.

73
00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:15.240
There's something quite satisfying
about deliberately dropping stitches.

74
00:06:15.440 --> 00:06:18.880
Block your piece to show off the pattern.

75
00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.080
This stitch pattern looks great on both

76
00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:25.240
sides, opening lots
of design possibilities.

77
00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.800
Please give it a try and add
a designer touch to your next project.

